    "content": "A recent analysis published by Goobjoog examines the lasting impact of European colonialism on Somalia, particularly the divisions created by the imposition of foreign languages and the subsequent dispute over a national script. The report notes that colonial powers, upon leaving Africa, left behind not only contested borders but also their languages and cultures, which spread across the continent. Each African nation adopted the language of its colonizer, and Somalia was partitioned among French, Italian, British, and Ethiopian interests, each promoting its own language in the territory it controlled.\n\nThe report highlights that Italy, for instance, sought to make Italian the language of government institutions and the public, including in education. This policy, along with similar approaches by other colonial powers, contributed to linguistic fragmentation. The eventual adoption of a unified Somali script in 1972 was a significant step, but the legacy of colonial language policies continues to influence debates over language and identity in Somalia and among its diaspora.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this history is particularly relevant as many second- and third-generation Somalis navigate multiple linguistic identities. The choice of script and language in education and administration remains a sensitive topic, often tied to broader questions of national unity and cultural preservation. The report underscores how colonial decisions made decades ago still shape contemporary discussions about language policy in Somalia and in diaspora communities.\n\nThe analysis also points to the broader African context, where colonial languages often remain official languages, sometimes at the expense of indigenous ones. In Somalia's case, the division into different colonial spheres meant that different regions were exposed to different European languages, complicating efforts to establish a standardized national language after independence. This historical backdrop is essential for understanding current debates over language and identity.\n\nUltimately, the report suggests that the colonial legacy of linguistic division is a factor in ongoing conflicts and identity politics in Somalia. For the diaspora, engaging with this history can inform efforts to preserve Somali language and culture while also navigating life in multilingual societies. The full report provides further details on how these colonial policies were implemented and their long-term effects.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/09/13/colaaddii-soomaaliya-uga-timid-qoriddii-farta-soomaaliga/",
    "source": "goobjoog.com",
