    "id": "news-1789306369619-6",
    "title": "BRICS Nations Adopt New Delhi Declaration Urging De-escalation in Middle East",
    "original_title": "BRICS oo Isku Raacay Baaq Lagu Dejinayo Dagaalka Bariga Dhexe",
    "slug": "brics-nations-adopt-new-delhi-declaration-urging-de-escalation-in-middle-east",
    "summary": "BRICS member states have jointly approved the New Delhi Declaration, expressing deep concern over rising tensions in the Middle East and calling for restraint and diplomatic solutions.",
    "content": "The member countries of the BRICS group have jointly endorsed the New Delhi Declaration, voicing serious alarm over the escalating conflict and tensions in the Middle East. The statement urged all parties involved to exercise full restraint and to refrain from any actions that could further inflame the situation. BRICS leaders reiterated their position that international disputes should be resolved through dialogue, consultation, and diplomatic means.\n\nThe declaration also emphasized the need to protect civilians, respect the sovereignty of states, and pursue solutions based on multilateral cooperation. This move comes as the Middle East continues to experience heightened instability, with recent outbreaks of violence drawing international attention.\n\nBRICS, which originally comprised Brazil, Russia, India, China, and South Africa, has expanded in recent years to include additional members such as Egypt, Ethiopia, Iran, and the United Arab Emirates. The group has increasingly sought to present a unified voice on global security and political issues, often advocating for peaceful resolution of conflicts and greater representation of developing nations in international decision-making.\n\nThe New Delhi Declaration reflects a collective stance by some of the world's major emerging economies, many of which have direct or indirect interests in the Middle East. For the Somali diaspora, this development is significant because the Horn of Africa region is closely tied to Middle Eastern security dynamics. Instability in the Middle East can affect trade routes, remittance flows, and regional stability, all of which have direct implications for Somali communities both in the diaspora and in Somalia.\n\nFurthermore, several BRICS members, including Egypt and the UAE, are actively engaged in Horn of Africa affairs, and their positions on Middle East conflicts can influence broader diplomatic and economic engagement with Somalia. The declaration's call for multilateral solutions and respect for sovereignty resonates with Somali stakeholders who advocate for non-interference and peaceful dispute resolution in the region.\n\nAs the international community watches the situation unfold, the BRICS statement adds to growing pressure for de-escalation. Whether it will lead to concrete action remains uncertain, but it signals a coordinated effort by major emerging powers to address one of the world's most volatile conflicts.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/09/13/brics-oo-isku-raacay-baaq-lagu-dejinayo-dagaalka-bariga-dhexe/",
